1. What is the purpose of the try and catch blocks?
They prevent the program from crashing unexpectedly when an error occurs. The ⁠try⁠ block holds risky code, while the ⁠catch⁠ block handles the error gracefully.  
2. Explain the difference between throw and throws.
⁠throw⁠ is used to explicitly create and trigger an exception, whereas ⁠throws⁠ is declared in a method signature to indicate that the method can throw an exception.  
3. What is the purpose of the finally block?
It executes mandatory code regardless of whether an exception occurred or not (such as closing resources like the scanner).  
4. Why is a custom exception useful in this application?
It enables the application to define specific, clear error messages tailored directly to the business rules of the exam registration system